WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:08.760
Iraqi Kurdistan's independence
referendum may seem like the culmination

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:12.900
of a century long dream. If it were to
happen, the outcome would be clear. But

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:16.320
why is the United States and the
international community so opposed? The

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:20.400
problem is determining what is Kurdistan.
The world recognizes Erbil, Sulaymaniyah,  and

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:23.760
Dohuk as Kurdish, but the referendum
includes other areas claimed by the

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:27.779
Iraqis. That means an immediate fight
over borders. Then there's the economy.

00:00:27.779 --> 00:00:31.380
Kurdistan is twenty billion dollars in
debt. It has less oil than the rest of

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:35.280
Iraq, and landlocked, it would be at its
neighbors mercy. After independence, it

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:39.719
will not only lose its 17 percent claim
to Iraq's revenues, but Baghdad will also

00:00:39.719 --> 00:00:43.410
demand it pay a share of Iraq's debt.
Simply put, it's not going to be able to

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:46.680
pay its teachers, soldiers, and
bureaucrats. The biggest problem will be

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:50.129
its neighbors. Both Turkey and Iran fear
the precedent independence would have

00:00:50.129 --> 00:00:53.879
upon their own Kurdish minorities. Iran
can destabilize its new neighbor, and

00:00:53.879 --> 00:00:57.090
Turkey can send in troops. Political
divisions among the Kurdish Peshmerga

00:00:57.090 --> 00:01:01.460
further undermine hope for stability. So
here's the question for Washington:

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:06.680
Americans might sympathize with Kurdish aspirations, but will America fund and fight for them?

